            <div xml:id="d634">

                <pb facs="0821" n="731" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg731"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.6363/72: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-09-12T14:00:00+01:00">September 12, 1934—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 12—9:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>519. Department’s 358, September 11, 8 p.m. Text of Department’s telegram 347,
                    August 31, noon,<anchor n="32" xml:id="d634-f1"/> was read to the Chief of the
                    Far Eastern [Department] September 1st who after expressing thanks remarked “it
                    is all right as far as it goes” and later mentioned that he feared it might be
                    difficult “to bring the small American companies into line.”</p>
                <p>He said that after talking the matter over with the interested departments he
                    would again get in touch with the Embassy.</p>
                <p>Nothing written or oral has since been received from the Foreign Office.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
